[Music]  how does a person  who escapes from  slavery create a literary masterpiece  seven years later language for douglas  first came of course orally  he's taught to read and write first by  sophia old in baltimore when he was  seven eight nine years old finding the  colombian order was absolutely crucial  that book was a true treasure to him and  then he attends uh four different  churches in baltimore when he's a  teenager  he was learning some  level of homolytics preaching before he  ever left baltimore and then we also  know that you know when he's about 18  and he's stranded on the  freeland farm but it's there that he  developed  that he called it his band of brothers a  group that they'd go off on every sunday  and he would read out loud to them what  do you think the effect of being sent  from  the  relative freedom of  life in the city right to the plantation  to the fields well he was when he was 15  years old yeah he sent back to the  eastern shore  he was  desperate then he turned into  an angry uncontrollable teenager who has  seen the city  he's seen this relative freedom of  movement of imagination  and no doubt he had friends already  but it's like his imagination was  crushed he also studied avidly  newspapers particularly absolutely  william lloyd garrison's the liberator  so he's reading it like we read the new  york times he's reading the liberator  assiduously which is a weekly  uh every week when he's 20 21 22 years  old in new bedford even tells us he  tacked it on the wall when he worked on  that foundry right he'd work the bellows  with one arm and  read the liberator you know to the side  would it be anachronistic to call  frederick douglass america's first  celebrity uh he's certainly one of them  i mean pt barnum might want to claim  some piece of that  but douglas did become a creature of  great fame such that he was not only so  recognizable  especially after he appeared on the  cover of harpers  which was the 19th century version of  the cover of you know time magazine or  whatever there's also we have to  remember his travels were so ubiquitous  you know i went sat down and tried to  start tabulating the sheer number of  miles oh my god i was trying to come up  with the average winter tour was about  three to four thousand miles and so how  many years i quit  it's thousands hundreds of thousands of  miles but i do speculate in the book  that probably more americans heard  douglas speak  than any other american speaker and this  fame was a problem of course especially  in washington d.c especially in relation  to his family and especially in relation  to aspects of his personal life  it's a life where he has to eventually  calculate okay what do i say here for  public consumption and what can i say  that's not for public consumption  i do think douglas in fact i have no  doubt douglas felt the responsibility of  being this representative man  i also think he never stopped pursuing  who the hell am i  i am from the eastern shore i'm nobody  from the tuckahoe river but i'm look  where i am now  how did i get here how did a slave from  the tuckahoe river end up in speaking in  the greatest halls in edinburgh london  and and new york but he clearly was what  we would call a celebrity especially  later in life how important do you think  his legacy  was to him by the end oh very important  you don't write  life and times in 1881 and re  rewrite it with six more chapters in  1892 if you're not concerned about your  legacy he makes a lot of speeches toward  the end of his life where you can read  them as summations of his life  he does many many eulogies of other  abolitionists  there's no question he dies at a time  when  the great victories of emancipation the  war and reconstruction the remaking of  the united states is in great danger  southern redemption is complete  disfranchisement is spreading across the  south lynching is spreading across the  south he had every right to say we have  completely lost but he never really does  that  and again i think what sustained douglas  to the bitter end  was that he was the product of two deep  great traditions  one is the secular enlightenment  natural rights tradition which is  practically in everything he wrote and  spoke  and then the biblical tradition  faith  that somehow  you know no matter what happens to you  no matter if the temple is destroyed  no matter if your best efforts  are betrayed you can't give up  you have to stay with it you have to  have faith  [Music]  you

